var e=`city`,t=`/it-support/burlington/`,n=`https://itrapidsupport.com/it-support/burlington/`,r=`managed-it`,i=`Halton`,a=`Serving Burlington & the GTA`,o=`IT Support Burlington: IT Services and Managed IT for Local Businesses`,s=`Burlington IT support and IT services from IT Rapid Support give your business one IT company for everything: a 24/7 helpdesk your staff call directly, network support, cybersecurity, Microsoft 365 administration, restore-tested backups and IT consulting, under one flat monthly managed IT agreement. Support starts remotely from our head office at 7810 Keele St in Vaughan, with on-site visits scheduled across Burlington and Halton when the work needs hands on the equipment. One-off work without a contract is scoped in writing and billed by the hour, and the first assessment is free.`,c=`Burlington IT support and IT services from one IT company: a 24/7 helpdesk, network support, cybersecurity and IT consulting on one monthly fee. (289) 582-9930.`,l=null,u={image:null,alt:``},d=[{heading:`IT Services for Burlington Businesses`,body:[`Managed IT, cybersecurity, and 24/7 business tech support built around the needs of Burlington organizations.`],steps:[{title:`Managed IT & 24/7 Helpdesk`,body:`A round-the-clock helpdesk and proactive monitoring keep your Burlington users productive and catch issues before they cause downtime.`},{title:`Cybersecurity & Backup`,body:`Enterprise-grade security plus automated local and cloud backup with disaster recovery to protect your Burlington data from ransomware and failure.`},{title:`Cloud & Microsoft 365`,body:`Cloud migration, Microsoft 365, and Azure/AWS management to keep your Burlington team secure, mobile, and productive.`}]},{heading:`Burlington services in detail`,body:[`This page is the overview. Each of the pages below takes one service and explains how we deliver it in Burlington, who it is built for and what onboarding looks like.`],links:[{label:`IT helpdesk Burlington`,href:`/it-support/burlington/it-helpdesk/`},{label:`Network support Burlington`,href:`/it-support/burlington/network-support/`},{label:`Cybersecurity Burlington`,href:`/it-support/burlington/cybersecurity/`}],bullets:[`IT helpdesk Burlington: who answers when your staff call at any hour, what the help desk handles, and how on-site dispatch across Halton works.`,`Network support Burlington: firewalls, Wi-Fi, VPN, multi-site links and failover, and how we take over a network that has grown over the years.`,`Cybersecurity Burlington: Microsoft 365 and email security, EDR, 24/7 monitoring, production-floor segmentation and incident response.`]},{heading:`Burlington IT Helpdesk, Network Support and Cybersecurity: How the Services Fit Together`,body:[`Most Burlington businesses looking for IT support are really looking for three things at once: someone staff can call when something stops working, someone who looks after the network the business runs on, and someone accountable for security. We provide all three as one service, because in practice they are the same problem seen from different sides. A slow Microsoft 365 sign-in can be a network fault, a Wi-Fi complaint can turn out to be a security issue, and a helpdesk ticket about a strange email is often the first sign of a compromised mailbox.`,`Each has its own page with the detail. Our page on the IT helpdesk in Burlington explains who answers at any hour and how requests are handled; IT network support in Burlington covers firewalls, switches, Wi-Fi, VPN and failover internet; and cybersecurity in Burlington covers Microsoft 365 and email security, endpoint detection and response, and incident handling. Under a managed agreement all three sit under one monthly fee and one team that already knows your environment.`],links:[{label:`IT helpdesk in Burlington`,href:`/it-support/burlington/it-helpdesk/`},{label:`IT network support in Burlington`,href:`/it-support/burlington/network-support/`},{label:`cybersecurity in Burlington`,href:`/it-support/burlington/cybersecurity/`}]},{heading:`Choosing an IT Company in Burlington: Questions to Ask Before You Sign`,body:[`Burlington businesses comparing IT companies usually receive quotes that look alike on paper: a price per user and a list of services. The differences are in the details, so put the same questions to every provider. Who answers the phone after hours, a technician or a message service? Is security, meaning endpoint protection, multi-factor authentication, email filtering and monitoring, inside the base price or sold as an add-on? Are backups restore-tested, and how often? Who will own the administrator accounts for your Microsoft 365 tenant, your domain and your firewall?`,`Ask as well how on-site work reaches you. We are open about ours: the office is at 7810 Keele St in Vaughan, most requests are resolved remotely, and technicians are scheduled to Burlington when a fault needs hands on equipment. We do not publish a guaranteed response time; before you sign we explain how visits are scheduled. Our guide to comparing managed IT quotes lists what should be written into any agreement, whichever IT company you choose.`],links:[{label:`guide to comparing managed IT quotes`,href:`/resources/how-to-compare-managed-it-quotes/`}]},{heading:`IT Consulting in Burlington: Assessments, Projects and Second Opinions`,body:[`Not every Burlington business needs an ongoing agreement. Some need an IT consultant for a defined piece of work: a review of a Microsoft 365 tenant that grew without a plan, a network assessment before an office move or expansion, a written answer to a customer's security questionnaire or a cyber insurance renewal, or a second opinion on a quote from another provider. We scope this work in writing before it starts and bill it by the hour.`,`The result is something you can use with or without us: written findings in priority order, what each fix involves, and the cost of doing it and of leaving it. For businesses that want the same thinking applied every quarter, including budgets, warranty and support dates and a security roadmap, our vCIO and IT strategy service puts it on a regular schedule.`],links:[{label:`vCIO and IT strategy service`,href:`/services/vcio-it-strategy/`}]},{heading:`Tech Support for Burlington Clinics, Dental Offices and Professional Firms`,body:[`A large share of Burlington's small businesses are clinics, dental and physiotherapy practices, and accounting, law, insurance and financial planning offices: in downtown buildings around Brant Street, in plazas along Fairview Street, Guelph Line, Walkers Line and Appleby Line, and in medical offices near Joseph Brant Hospital on Lakeshore Road. Most have no one on staff responsible for IT, so tech support means the person at the front desk calling us when the practice-management system will not open or the scanner stops sending to email.`,`For these offices the work that matters is mostly configuration rather than equipment: multi-factor authentication on every account, encrypted laptops and workstations, practice-management and accounting software kept patched, client and patient records backed up and restore-tested, and front-desk, staff and guest Wi-Fi kept apart. Clinics holding personal health information answer to PHIPA, and every business holding personal information answers to PIPEDA's breach-reporting and record-keeping rules, so we keep the sign-in and audit logs that show what happened if something goes wrong.`]},{heading:`Burlington IT Services: What Your Business Gets`,body:[`Our Burlington IT services run as a complete outsourced IT department: a 24/7 helpdesk your staff can call any hour, proactive monitoring and patching that catches failing hardware and unpatched vulnerabilities early, Microsoft 365 administration, backup monitoring with tested recovery, and layered cybersecurity — managed firewalls, endpoint protection, MFA, and managed detection and response. On-site support covers Aldershot, Brant, Millcroft, and the rest of Burlington and Halton.`,`Everything is delivered under one agreement with fixed monthly pricing, so a Burlington business gets enterprise-grade coverage without hiring an internal team or juggling separate vendors for support, security, and cloud.`]},{heading:`Outsourcing IT Support in Burlington`,body:[`Burlington businesses typically outsource IT for three reasons: coverage an internal hire cannot match (24/7, no vacations, no single point of failure), security that is actually managed rather than installed and forgotten, and predictable cost. A managed agreement replaces surprise break-fix invoices with one fixed monthly fee that scales with headcount.`,`If you are evaluating IT outsourcing in Burlington or comparing providers across Halton, the managed IT cost guide and provider-selection checklist in the guides below are the fastest starting point — or call (289) 582-9930 and we will assess your current environment.`]},{heading:`24/7 IT Helpdesk for Burlington Businesses`,body:[`Our helpdesk answers around the clock. Every ticket reaches a technician who can remote in immediately, and work that needs hands on hardware is dispatched from our Vaughan office. Around the helpdesk runs the maintenance layer that keeps ticket volume down in the first place: monitoring of servers, workstations and network devices, patching on a stated cadence, Microsoft 365 administration and backup verification.`,`The honest measure of a helpdesk is what happens to the awkward tickets. Password resets are easy. The intermittent fault nobody can reproduce, the application that breaks only for one department, the problem that turns out to be the internet circuit rather than the software — those are the ones that get quietly abandoned elsewhere, and the ones we track to a written cause rather than closing on a reboot. How tickets are triaged, verified and dispatched is set out on our page for IT helpdesk in Burlington.`],links:[{label:`IT helpdesk in Burlington`,href:`/it-support/burlington/it-helpdesk/`}]},{heading:`Why Burlington Businesses Look for a Local IT Partner`,body:[`Burlington had 186,948 residents at the 2021 census, up 2.0 per cent from 183,314 in 2016 — the slowest growth of the three Halton municipalities by a wide margin, against Oakville at 10.3 per cent and Milton's well-documented expansion. The reason is geography rather than demand: the main urban area sits south of the Parkway Belt and Highway 407, and the land north of that and north of Aldershot is agricultural, rural residential and conservation, with the Niagara Escarpment behind it. Burlington is close to built out.`,`That produces a different kind of business than a growth suburb does. Burlington companies tend to be established, to have been in the same building for a long time, and to expand by intensifying rather than relocating. The IT consequence is layered infrastructure: a network that has been added to over fifteen years, a server that was virtualised once and never revisited, cabling from three separate decades. The work is rarely a greenfield build. It is untangling something that grew, which requires documenting it before touching it.`]},{heading:`Burlington Employment Areas and Business Districts We Cover`,body:[`The Queen Elizabeth Way and Highway 403 run concurrently through most of Burlington, with the 407 ETR across the north and Highway 6 forming the boundary with Hamilton. The employment land follows those corridors: the Harvester Road and North Service Road band running parallel to the QEW is the city's main industrial and commercial spine, with the Mainway and Upper Middle Road corridor above it, and the Appleby Line, Walkers Line and Guelph Line arterials running north to south through both.`,`Downtown Burlington around Brant Street and the waterfront carries the professional services and hospitality base, Aldershot sits at the western end towards Hamilton along Plains Road, and Burloak Drive is the boundary with Oakville. We cover all of it, and across the boundaries too: IT support in Oakville to the east, IT support in Milton north across Derry Road (see also Milton cybersecurity services), and IT support in Hamilton to the southwest. Multi-site businesses are managed as one environment under one agreement.`],links:[{label:`IT support in Oakville`,href:`/it-support/oakville/`},{label:`IT support in Milton`,href:`/it-support/milton/`},{label:`Milton cybersecurity services`,href:`/it-support/milton/cybersecurity/`},{label:`IT support in Hamilton`,href:`/it-support/hamilton/`}]},{heading:`Managed IT Services in Burlington: What Is Actually Included`,body:[`A managed agreement covers the recurring work that keeps an environment healthy: a 24/7 helpdesk your staff contact directly, monitoring of servers, workstations and network devices, patch management, Microsoft 365 administration, backup monitoring with tested restores, and layered security including managed firewalls, endpoint protection, multi-factor authentication and managed detection and response.`,`The part worth reading in any provider's agreement, ours included, is what sits outside the monthly fee. Projects, hardware, third-party licensing and after-hours on-site attendance are commonly billed separately, which is reasonable; discovering it at the invoice is not. Ask any provider to put on one page what is included and what is not. If that page is hard to produce, that difficulty is your answer.`]},{heading:`Cybersecurity Services for Burlington Businesses`,body:[`Security is built in layers rather than purchased as a product: managed firewalls with segmented networks, endpoint detection and response on every device, email filtering and impersonation protection, multi-factor authentication enforced rather than offered, privileged accounts kept separate from daily-use accounts, and monitored logging so that an incident has a timeline instead of a guess. None of this is exotic; what separates an environment that survives an incident from one that does not is whether the basics were applied everywhere or only where somebody remembered.`,`For most Burlington businesses the realistic threat is not a targeted intrusion but a convincing invoice-redirection email, a credential phished from a personal device, or a supplier account compromised before yours. Those are addressed through identity, email configuration and monitoring rather than through hardware, which is how our managed security service is ordered. How we run those controls for Halton plants, warehouses and downtown professional firms is set out on our page for cybersecurity services in Burlington.`],links:[{label:`managed security service`,href:`/services/managed-security/`},{label:`cybersecurity services in Burlington`,href:`/it-support/burlington/cybersecurity/`}]},{heading:`Data Backup and Disaster Recovery in Burlington`,body:[`A backup nobody has restored from is a belief rather than a control. We run local and cloud copies, monitor every job, and test restores on a schedule so the recovery time is measured rather than assumed. Retention is set against your real obligations, and copies are held so that ransomware reaching the live environment does not reach the backup with it.`,`Two business decisions set the design and most of the cost: how much data you can afford to lose, and how long you can afford to be down. They are not technical questions, and nothing sensible can be designed until they are written down. Our business continuity and disaster recovery service covers the full approach, including what a tested restore record should actually contain.`],links:[{label:`business continuity and disaster recovery service`,href:`/services/business-continuity-disaster-recovery/`}]},{heading:`Microsoft 365, Azure and Cloud Services for Burlington Companies`,body:[`Most Burlington businesses are already in Microsoft 365 and using a fraction of what they pay for. The licensing is bought, the security features inside it are off, sharing is at its default, and nobody reads the audit log. Extracting value from a subscription you already hold is almost always cheaper than buying something new, so it is where we start rather than where we finish.`,`From there the work is a tenant baseline done properly: conditional access, retention matched to your obligations, external sharing made deliberate, device compliance, and administrator accounts separated from everyday ones. Where a physical server is still doing real work we say so — not every workload belongs in the cloud, and a migration recommended without an assessment is a sales pitch. Our Microsoft 365 and Azure migration page describes how we scope it.`],links:[{label:`Microsoft 365 and Azure migration`,href:`/services/microsoft-365-azure-migration/`}]},{heading:`Network Support and Management for Burlington Offices`,body:[`In a city where businesses stay in their buildings, the network is usually the most layered part of the environment. A switch added during one expansion, an access point positioned for convenience during another, a firewall rule opened for a project that finished years ago, cabling from three different eras behind the same wall plate. We document what exists first, replace what is genuinely past support rather than merely old, separate guest from staff traffic properly, and monitor the links so degradation shows up before it becomes an outage.`,`Wireless in the Harvester Road and North Service Road industrial stock behaves nothing like wireless in a downtown Brant Street office, and neither behaves like the coverage map a vendor produces from a floor plan. We survey buildings as they are used and loaded. Our network management service covers the ongoing side of that work.`],links:[{label:`network management service`,href:`/services/network-management/`}]},{heading:`IT Network Support in Burlington: What We Look After`,body:[`IT network support in Burlington is the part of our managed service that covers everything between your internet provider and the devices your staff use. Day to day that means firewall management (rules reviewed, firmware patched, logs monitored), switch and Wi-Fi administration, VLANs that keep guest, staff, phone and production traffic apart, VPN and remote access for staff working from home, and monitoring on every circuit and core device so a failing port or a degrading line raises an alert before it becomes a call from the front desk.`,`The work differs by building. A multi-tenant office near Brant Street or along Fairview Street often shares a riser and has limited control over the telecom room, so we plan around what the landlord allows. A unit in the Harvester Road or North Service Road employment lands usually has metal racking, a warehouse floor and a yard to cover, which changes access point placement entirely. Businesses along Plains Road in Aldershot or in the newer Alton Village plazas often run a second site in Oakville or Hamilton, and both networks need to behave as one, with the same sign-in, file access and security policy at each.`,`When something does fail, the fix is usually remote: we can see the firewall, switches and access points from our monitoring and change configuration on the spot. Hardware faults, cabling and new installs get an on-site visit from our Vaughan office. How we take over, document and run a network here is set out on our page for IT network support in Burlington, our guide to IT network support explains what a properly run network includes, and the ongoing service is described on our network management page. For a review of your current Burlington network, call (289) 582-9930.`],links:[{label:`IT network support in Burlington`,href:`/it-support/burlington/network-support/`},{label:`IT network support`,href:`/resources/it-network-support-gta/`},{label:`network management`,href:`/services/network-management/`}]},{heading:`IT Consulting, vCIO and Budget Planning for Burlington Businesses`,body:[`An established business with aging infrastructure has a specific budgeting problem: everything is working, nothing is urgent, and the replacement bill is accumulating quietly behind the scenes until several things fall due at once. That is the standard Burlington pattern, and it is what a lifecycle plan exists to prevent. Our vCIO and IT strategy service puts it on a schedule.`,`The output is practical: an asset and lifecycle register showing what is out of warranty and out of vendor support, a security roadmap with the order written down, a budget a finance team can read without translation, and a review cadence where the plan is corrected against what actually happened. For businesses facing customer due-diligence questionnaires or insurance renewals, it also becomes the place the evidence lives, so answers are not reassembled from memory each time.`],links:[{label:`vCIO and IT strategy service`,href:`/services/vcio-it-strategy/`}]},{heading:`IT for Manufacturing, Food Processing and Distribution in Burlington`,body:[`Burlington's economic strength is that no single employer or sector dominates it. The leading industrial sectors by employment are food processing, packaging, electronics, motor vehicle and transportation, business services, chemical and pharmaceutical, and environmental. The largest private employers include Fearmans Pork, Cogeco, Evertz Microsystems, Boehringer Ingelheim and EMC2, while the City of Burlington, the two Halton school boards and Joseph Brant Hospital anchor the public side. The Port of Hamilton on Burlington Bay sits immediately to the west.`,`Production environments are not offices with machines in them. Food processing brings washdown areas, temperature monitoring and traceability records that must survive an audit. Packaging and electronics bring line equipment running software nobody is allowed to patch casually. The approach that works is segmentation and honesty: keep production networks separate from office networks, know precisely which machines cannot be touched and why, and put compensating controls around them rather than pretending the risk is not there.`]},{heading:`Switching IT Providers in Burlington: The Four Stages`,body:[`Discovery comes before anything is signed: what hardware, licences, domains, backups and accounts exist, and who holds the keys to each. Documentation follows, where all of it is recorded and the gaps are named out loud rather than discovered later. Transition puts monitoring, patching, backup and security tooling in place alongside the outgoing arrangement rather than after it, so there is never a period with nobody watching.`,`Handover is the stage that gets skipped. Administrative credentials, domain registrations, Microsoft tenant ownership and licence agreements are confirmed in writing to be in your name and under your control. Ownership of your own domain, tenant and backups is not a courtesy extended by a provider, and reluctance to confirm it in writing tells you what you need to know about the relationship.`]},{heading:`On-Site IT Support Across Burlington and Halton Region`,body:[`Most faults are resolved remotely, which is genuinely faster rather than a way of avoiding the drive. When hands are needed — a failed switch, a cabling fault, an office fit-out, a hardware refresh — technicians are dispatched from our Vaughan office along the 407 or the QEW. That work is scheduled rather than promised against a response time we have not measured for your address.`,`Burlington has three GO stations on the Lakeshore West line — Appleby, Burlington and Aldershot — with Via Rail service at Aldershot, and it sits roughly in the geographic centre of the Golden Horseshoe. For a business with staff distributed across Halton, Hamilton and the western GTA, that access is an advantage worth designing around rather than working against: remote-first support, identity that works from anywhere, and on-site visits planned rather than reactive.`]},{heading:`Industries We Support Across Burlington`,body:[`Manufacturers, food processors and packaging operations, where the production floor and the office genuinely differ and only one of them can be managed like an office. Professional services and financial firms downtown and along Fairview, where the file history is the business and client due diligence arrives on a schedule. Healthcare and care operators, where availability and privacy obligations sit on the same systems.`,`Also construction and trades, where the office is wherever the project is; distribution and logistics along the Harvester and North Service corridor; and retail and hospitality downtown and at Mapleview and Burlington Centre, where payment systems, guest wireless and staff turnover all land on the same network. What these have in common is not a product requirement but an accountability one: somebody has to own the whole environment rather than the parts that are convenient.`]},{heading:`The Escarpment, the Parkway Belt and Where Connectivity Reaches`,body:[`Burlington's main urban area sits south of the Parkway Belt and Highway 407. North of that line, and north of Aldershot, the land is agricultural, rural residential and conservation, with the Niagara Escarpment behind it and Mount Nemo rising roughly two hundred metres above lake level. It is a genuine physical boundary rather than a planning convention, and it decides what is straightforward to connect and what is not.`,`Down in the serviced corridors — Harvester Road, the North Service Road, Fairview, Mainway and the downtown core — connectivity options are good and the usual advice applies: order early, because a business circuit lead time is measured in weeks or months rather than days. North of the Parkway Belt and out along the escarpment the picture changes address by address, and what a provider quotes for a postal code is frequently not what is available at a specific driveway.`,`Where the connection is the real constraint, we design around it rather than talking past it: cellular failover so one fault does not stop the day, remotely manageable equipment so a stuck router is not a site visit, local caching for large files, and cloud services chosen for how they behave on an imperfect link rather than on a demonstration. Checking what genuinely reaches the address before a lease is signed is the cheapest hour anyone spends on this.`]},{heading:`Succession, Ownership Change and IT Due Diligence in Burlington`,body:[`Burlington skews older than the national average — a median age of 43.3 at the 2016 census with 19.2 per cent of residents aged 65 or over, against a national figure of 16.9 per cent — and its business base skews the same way: established companies, long-tenured owners, and a genuine pipeline of ownership transitions. That produces a category of IT work that growth suburbs simply do not generate as often.`,`Ownership change puts an environment under a kind of scrutiny it has never faced. A buyer's advisers ask who owns the domain name, who holds the Microsoft tenant, whether software licensing is genuinely transferable, where customer data lives and under what agreement, whether there has ever been a breach, and whether the person who set all this up fifteen years ago is an employee, a contractor or a relative. Answering those from memory during a transaction is expensive, and the answers are frequently unwelcome.`,`The useful time to fix it is well before anyone is at the table. Confirming that domains, tenants, licences and backups are registered to the company rather than to an individual, documenting the environment properly, and clearing up undocumented arrangements is inexpensive in normal times and awkward under a deadline. We do this work as a standing part of a vCIO engagement rather than as a special project, precisely because it is worth the most when nobody is expecting to need it.`]},{heading:`Hardware Lifecycle and Procurement for Burlington Businesses`,body:[`In a business base this established, the lifecycle register is usually the single most valuable document we produce. What is in warranty, what is out of warranty but still supported, what has passed vendor end-of-support entirely, and what the replacement will cost in which year. Supported versus unsupported is the line that matters — a six-year-old workstation still receiving security patches is manageable, and a four-year-old appliance the vendor has abandoned is not, whatever its condition.`,`The pattern that hurts Burlington companies specifically is synchronised aging. Everything was bought at once during a good year, everything worked for a decade, and then everything falls due together. Spreading replacement deliberately across budget years, starting with whatever has passed end-of-support rather than whatever is loudest, converts a capital shock into a line item.`,`Production and processing environments get counted separately from office equipment, because the duty on them is different and so is their realistic life. And we will tell you when not to buy: a meaningful share of performance complaints turn out to be network, storage or configuration problems wearing a hardware costume, where replacing endpoints leaves the cause untouched and adds an invoice.`]},{heading:`IT Services in Burlington: Managed Agreement or Hourly Support`,body:[`There are two ways to work with us. A managed IT agreement is a flat monthly fee covering the recurring work: helpdesk, monitoring, patching, Microsoft 365 administration, backup testing and security. Hourly support is for businesses that only need help occasionally, and it is billed by the hour, with each piece of work scoped in writing before it starts.`,`Hourly support suits a small Burlington office with everything in Microsoft 365, no server and a few problems a year. It stops being the cheaper option once the preventive work is not happening: backups nobody has restored, patches applied when someone remembers, a former employee's account still active. Many clients start with the free assessment or a one-off project and decide afterwards; if hourly is genuinely enough, we will say so.`]},{heading:`IT Support for Aldershot, Downtown Brant Street and the QEW and 407 Corridor`,body:[`Downtown Burlington along Brant Street, between the waterfront and the GO station area, is mostly professional offices, clinics, financial advisers and hospitality businesses, many of them in older or converted buildings. The IT work there is usually about a small team's identity, email and backups, plus the physical constraints of the building: where the network equipment can live, and how Wi-Fi gets through older walls. For staff who need a person on the phone rather than a ticket queue, see how our IT helpdesk in Burlington works.`,`Aldershot, at the western end of the city along Plains Road towards Hamilton, mixes small commercial strips with light industry, and businesses there often have staff or a second site across the Hamilton boundary. The QEW and 407 corridor, along Harvester Road, the North Service Road and up to the 407, is where the larger offices, warehouses and plants are, and where firewalls, switching, site links and failover matter most; that work is covered on our page for network support in Burlington. Whichever part of the city you are in, the same team and the same agreement apply.`],links:[{label:`IT helpdesk in Burlington`,href:`/it-support/burlington/it-helpdesk/`},{label:`network support in Burlington`,href:`/it-support/burlington/network-support/`}]},{heading:`What to Have Ready Before Your First IT Assessment in Burlington`,body:[`The first assessment is free, and it goes faster when a few things are found in advance: admin access or your current provider's contact for Microsoft 365 or Google Workspace, who holds your domain registration and DNS, your internet provider account, a rough list of staff, devices and line-of-business software, and how backups are done and when one was last restored.`,`Burlington businesses that have been in the same building for years often discover that the domain is registered to a former employee or the firewall password lives with a previous provider. That is normal, and recovering ownership of those accounts is usually the first piece of work. The assessment ends with a written summary of what we found and what we would fix first, including the security gaps covered in more depth on our page for cybersecurity services in Burlington.`],links:[{label:`cybersecurity services in Burlington`,href:`/it-support/burlington/cybersecurity/`}]},{heading:`IT Support for Small Burlington Offices Without an IT Person`,body:[`A large share of Burlington businesses have fewer than twenty staff and no one whose job is IT, so it falls to the owner, the office manager or the most technical employee. The risks that matter most at that size are not exotic: an unprotected Microsoft 365 account, a backup that was never tested, a laptop with no encryption, and a domain nobody remembers renewing.`,`For those offices we keep the setup simple and documented: MFA on every account, managed and encrypted devices, email authentication on the domain, backups checked and restore-tested, and a single number staff can call. That can run under a managed agreement or start as hourly work, scoped in writing before anything is done.`]},{heading:`Check your IT risk before you call anyone`,body:[`Most Burlington businesses we speak to already suspect something is weak — they just cannot say which thing to fix first. Our free IT risk calculator answers that in a few minutes. It scores fifteen weighted security control areas — backups, multi-factor authentication, endpoint protection, admin accounts, patching, staff offboarding, email authentication, Microsoft 365 settings, encryption and more — then ranks your gaps by how much each one is costing your score and gives plain-English remediation for each.`,`There is no sign-up, no email gate and no report to wait for: it runs entirely in your browser and nothing you enter is sent to us or stored anywhere. If you want a person to look at the results with you afterwards, book a free IT Health Check — a 15-minute assessment with a written report, no obligation — or call (289) 582-9930.`,`Two narrower checks are worth running on their own. The first is our free email spoofing check, which reads your domain's published SPF, DKIM and DMARC records and tells you whether someone can send mail that looks like it came from you. That one matters more than most Burlington owners expect: when we reviewed the public DNS records of 118 GTA business domains, only 40% were fully protected against email spoofing. The second is our managed IT quote checker, which scores a provider's written proposal against 22 items so you can see what a quote leaves out before you sign it.`],links:[{label:`IT risk calculator`,href:`/it-risk-calculator/`},{label:`free IT Health Check`,href:`/it-health-check/`},{label:`free email spoofing check`,href:`/tools/email-spoof-check/`},{label:`reviewed the public DNS records of 118 GTA business domains`,href:`/resources/gta-email-spoofing-study-2026/`},{label:`managed IT quote checker`,href:`/tools/it-quote-checker/`}]},{heading:`Sectors we support in Burlington`,body:[`The service is the same underneath, but the shape of it is not. A firm holding privileged client files needs different controls from a warehouse running scanners on a shift pattern, and the maintenance window that suits an office is useless to a clinic that sees patients all day. We publish a page for each sector setting out what actually changes, including IT support for construction and trades, IT support for professional services firms, managed IT for financial services firms, managed IT for dental practices and IT services for nonprofits and charities.`,`If your sector is not listed, the answer is almost always still yes: call (289) 582-9930 and we will tell you plainly whether we are the right fit for what you run in Burlington.`],links:[{label:`IT support for construction and trades`,href:`/industries/construction/`},{label:`IT support for professional services firms`,href:`/industries/professional-services/`},{label:`managed IT for financial services firms`,href:`/industries/financial-services/`},{label:`managed IT for dental practices`,href:`/industries/dental/`},{label:`IT services for nonprofits and charities`,href:`/industries/nonprofit/`}]},{heading:`Areas We Serve Around Burlington`,body:[`Fast remote support and scheduled on-site service across Burlington and nearby communities.`],eyebrow:`Local, On-Demand Support`,bullets:[`Aldershot`,`Brant`,`Alton Village`,`Millcroft`,`Roseland`,`Tyandaga`]},{heading:`Burlington IT Support FAQs`,faq:[{q:`How can your managed IT support help my Burlington business?`,a:`We provide proactive monitoring and maintenance, a 24/7 helpdesk, network and endpoint management, cloud services, data backup and recovery, and comprehensive cybersecurity, serving as your complete IT department so you can focus on your core business.`},{q:`What cybersecurity do you recommend for small businesses in Burlington?`,a:`For Burlington small businesses we recommend a layered approach: enterprise-grade firewalls, endpoint protection, email security with anti-phishing, MFA, security awareness training, managed detection and response, and regular vulnerability scanning, packaged to make enterprise-level protection affordable.`},{q:`Do you offer on-site IT support in Burlington?`,a:`Yes. Alongside immediate remote support, our certified technicians provide scheduled and emergency on-site support across Burlington and the wider Halton and GTA region.`},{q:`What does IT network support cover for a Burlington business?`,a:`The firewall, switches, Wi-Fi access points, internet circuits and the cabling that connects them. In practice that means keeping firmware current, reviewing firewall rules so old openings are closed, separating guest, staff and production traffic, monitoring each link so a degrading circuit is caught before it drops, and keeping a network diagram that matches what is actually in the comms room. It is included in our managed IT agreement rather than sold as a separate contract.`},{q:`Can you add a backup internet connection to our Burlington office?`,a:`Yes, and for most offices it is the cheapest outage you will ever prevent. Burlington is served by more than one wired carrier in most commercial areas, plus cellular. We pair a primary circuit with a second connection from a different carrier or a cellular link, configure the firewall to fail over automatically, and monitor both so you know when you are running on the backup. What is available at a specific address on Harvester Road or in Aldershot varies, so we check before recommending anything.`},{q:`Do you have an office in Burlington?`,a:`No, and we would rather state it than imply a local storefront. Our office is at 7810 Keele St in Vaughan, reached along the 407 or the QEW. Most work is done remotely because that is genuinely the faster path, with on-site attendance dispatched from Vaughan when hands are needed. We do not publish a guaranteed response time, because a number published before anyone has looked at your address and environment is marketing rather than a commitment.`},{q:`Which parts of Burlington do you cover?`,a:`All of it. The Harvester Road and North Service Road industrial corridor along the QEW, the Mainway and Upper Middle Road band above it, downtown Burlington around Brant Street and the waterfront, Aldershot and Plains Road at the western end, and the Appleby, Walkers and Guelph Line corridors running north to south. Businesses with sites in Burlington plus Oakville, Milton or Hamilton are managed as one environment under one agreement rather than as separate accounts.`},{q:`What does managed IT actually include for a Burlington business?`,a:`A 24/7 helpdesk your staff contact directly, monitoring of servers, workstations and network devices, patch management on a stated cadence, Microsoft 365 administration, backup monitoring with tested restores, and layered security including managed firewalls, endpoint protection, multi-factor authentication and managed detection and response. Projects, hardware and third-party licensing are normally billed separately, which is standard practice; the time to see that list is before signing rather than at the first invoice.`},{q:`What actually drives the cost of IT support in Burlington?`,a:`Users and managed devices first, then the number of sites, then the shape of the environment: whether servers are still doing real work, whether there is a production floor to cover, how much of the security stack is included, and what recovery time you need. We do not publish a figure, because a price quoted before anyone has looked at your environment is a guess you would end up paying for. What is reasonable to demand from any provider is a written scope saying what is included, what is billed separately, and what happens at renewal.`},{q:`Our infrastructure has been added to for fifteen years. Where do you start?`,a:`With documentation, not replacement. The first job is an accurate record of what exists: every server and its purpose, every switch and access point, the firewall rules and why each one is there, the cabling, the licences, the domains, and who holds the administrative credentials. Almost every Burlington environment we inherit has at least one thing nobody can explain. Replacing hardware before that map exists is how a working system becomes a broken one, so we finish the map first and then rank the risks.`},{q:`Can you support a production or processing environment as well as the office?`,a:`Yes, and we treat them as different problems. Production networks are segmented away from office networks. Machines running software that cannot be patched casually are identified explicitly, with the reason recorded, and given compensating controls rather than being quietly ignored. Washdown areas, temperature monitoring and traceability records have physical and record-keeping requirements that an office design does not account for. The honest version of this work names what cannot be changed as clearly as what can.`},{q:`Do you work with businesses that already have an internal IT person?`,a:`Yes, and it is a large part of what we do. Co-managed IT keeps your internal person's relationships and application knowledge, and adds what is unreasonable to expect from one individual: 24/7 coverage, monitoring, patching, security tooling and escalation. It also removes the single point of failure, so vacation, illness and resignation stop being business continuity events. Our co-managed IT service page sets out how responsibilities are divided in writing.`},{q:`Can you help us answer a customer security questionnaire or an insurance renewal?`,a:`Yes. We map each question to what your environment actually does, implement what is genuinely missing rather than claiming it, and assemble evidence: policies that match reality, patch and backup reports, tested restore records with dates, access reviews and documented incident response. We will not confirm a control you do not have. That is the entire value of asking someone technical rather than answering the form optimistically and hoping it is never checked.`},{q:`How do you handle privacy obligations under PIPEDA and PHIPA?`,a:`We implement and operate the controls that support the obligation and are careful about the limits of that claim. Access control, encryption, logging with retention, tested restores, documented incident response and reviewed third-party access are the technical layer, documented so you can show your work. The obligation itself stays with your organization, and no IT provider can take it on. Any provider saying they deliver compliance is overselling what a technology vendor can do.`},{q:`We are moving or expanding within Burlington. When should IT get involved?`,a:`Before the lease is signed if possible, and certainly before the fit-out is designed. Connectivity and cabling are the two things that are expensive to fix afterwards. What service actually reaches a specific address varies more than people expect, particularly in the older industrial stock along Harvester Road, and business circuit lead times can run to months. Cabling, comms room location, power and cooling are inexpensive while the walls are open and disruptive once they are closed.`},{q:`What does 24/7 support mean in practice when your office is in Vaughan?`,a:`A technician answers at any hour and can remote into your systems immediately rather than logging a callback for the morning, and monitoring alerts reach a person rather than an inbox. It does not mean a van outside your Burlington building at 2 am, and we will not claim otherwise. On-site attendance is scheduled, and the environment is designed so that the number of faults genuinely requiring hands on hardware stays small.`},{q:`What happens in the first thirty days of working with you?`,a:`Discovery and documentation before anything is changed. We record what hardware, licences, domains, backups and accounts exist and who holds the keys, write it down, and name the gaps out loud. Monitoring, patching, backup verification and security tooling go in alongside whatever is currently running rather than after it, so there is no window with nobody watching. Anything needing replacement is presented with the reason and the risk attached, not as an assumed purchase.`},{q:`Do you support Apple hardware as well as Windows?`,a:`Yes. Mixed fleets are common in Burlington design, marketing and professional practices, and treating the Macs as an informal exception is exactly how they end up unmanaged, unpatched and outside the reporting. Both platforms get the same treatment: enrolment, device compliance, patching, endpoint protection and encryption, managed from one place so the reporting covers the whole estate rather than most of it.`},{q:`Can we get IT support in Burlington without a monthly contract?`,a:`Yes. One-off and occasional work for Burlington businesses is scoped in writing before it starts and billed by the hour, done remotely where possible and on site when hands are needed. If a pattern of repeat problems suggests a managed agreement would cost less, we will show you the numbers, but there is no obligation to sign one.`},{q:`What IT services do you offer Burlington businesses?`,a:`Managed IT is the core: a 24/7 helpdesk, monitoring and patching, Microsoft 365 and cloud administration, monitored backups with tested restores, and security in the base fee. Alongside it we handle project and one-off work such as Microsoft 365 migrations, network takeovers and documentation, office moves and fit-outs, hardware lifecycle planning, vCIO and budget planning, IT due diligence before a sale or acquisition, and investigation after a suspected mailbox compromise. Everything is delivered from our Vaughan head office.`},{q:`What should we have ready for a first IT assessment in Burlington?`,a:`Whatever you can find of the following, and a note of what you cannot: admin access or your current provider's contact for Microsoft 365 or Google Workspace, who holds your domain registration and DNS, your internet provider account details, a rough list of staff, devices and business software, how backups are done and when one was last restored, and any firewall, switch or Wi-Fi equipment and who installed it. In an established Burlington business some of this will be missing; finding the gaps is part of the assessment.`},{q:`How do your Burlington IT helpdesk, network support and cybersecurity services fit together?`,a:`They are one service delivered by one team. The helpdesk is where your staff report a problem, network support covers the firewall, switches, Wi-Fi and internet connections the business runs on, and cybersecurity covers Microsoft 365 and email security, endpoint protection and monitoring. Under a managed agreement all three are covered by the same monthly fee, so a fault that crosses from one area to another is still one ticket.`},{q:`Do you provide IT consulting in Burlington without a monthly contract?`,a:`Yes. Defined pieces of work, such as a Microsoft 365 tenant review, a network assessment before an office move, help answering a customer security questionnaire or a second opinion on another provider's quote, are scoped in writing first and billed by the hour. You receive written findings in priority order that you can use with or without us.`},{q:`Do you provide tech support for clinics and professional offices in Burlington?`,a:`Yes. We support clinics, dental and physiotherapy practices, and accounting, law, insurance and financial planning offices across Burlington, including downtown around Brant Street and the plazas along Fairview Street, Guelph Line, Walkers Line and Appleby Line. Most requests are handled remotely through the helpdesk, with on-site visits scheduled from our Vaughan office when equipment needs attention.`}]},{heading:`IT Guides for Burlington Businesses`,body:[`Plain-language guides from our team on choosing and running managed IT.`],links:[{label:`IT Support Across Halton Region: What to Expect`,href:`/resources/it-support-halton-region/`},{label:`IT Support Services in the GTA: A Buyer's Guide`,href:`/resources/it-support-services-gta-buyers-guide/`},{label:`How to Choose a Managed IT Provider in Toronto`,href:`/resources/choosing-managed-it-provider-toronto/`},{label:`IT outsourcing in Burlington`,href:`/resources/it-outsourcing-burlington/`}],bullets:[`IT Support Across Halton Region: What to Expect`,`IT Support Services in the GTA: A Buyer's Guide`,`How to Choose a Managed IT Provider in Toronto`,`More Guides for Burlington`,`IT outsourcing in Burlington`]},{heading:`Managed IT Services Near Burlington`,links:[{label:`Managed IT Services Oakville`,href:`/it-support/oakville/`},{label:`IT Support Hamilton`,href:`/it-support/hamilton/`},{label:`Managed IT in Mississauga`,href:`/it-support/mississauga/`},{label:`IT Services in Milton`,href:`/it-support/milton/`},{label:`Managed IT Services North York`,href:`/it-support/north-york/`},{label:`IT Support Etobicoke`,href:`/it-support/etobicoke/`}],bullets:[`Managed IT Services Oakville`,`IT Support Hamilton`,`Managed IT in Mississauga`,`IT Services in Milton`,`Managed IT Services North York`,`IT Support Etobicoke`]}],f={label:`Get a Free Consultation`,href:`/contact/`},p=`/it-support/burlington/it-helpdesk/./it-support/burlington/network-support/./it-support/burlington/cybersecurity/./resources/how-to-compare-managed-it-quotes/./services/vcio-it-strategy/./it-support/oakville/./it-support/milton/./it-support/milton/cybersecurity/./it-support/hamilton/./services/managed-security/./services/business-continuity-disaster-recovery/./services/microsoft-365-azure-migration/./services/network-management/./resources/it-network-support-gta/./it-risk-calculator/./it-health-check/./tools/email-spoof-check/./resources/gta-email-spoofing-study-2026/./tools/it-quote-checker/./industries/construction/./industries/professional-services/./industries/financial-services/./industries/dental/./industries/nonprofit/./resources/it-support-halton-region/./resources/it-support-services-gta-buyers-guide/./resources/choosing-managed-it-provider-toronto/./resources/it-outsourcing-burlington/./it-support/mississauga/./it-support/north-york/./it-support/etobicoke/./services/`.split(`.`),m=[`Keeps ITRS own-study statistics (e.g. "118 GTA domains, 40% protected" / "481 domains, 20.6% DMARC") - confirm the figures match the published studies.`,`Keeps "certified technicians" wording - which certification? Confirm or soften.`,`Keeps "15-minute" free IT Health Check claim - confirm the format.`],h={type:e,path:t,sourceUrl:n,practice:r,region:i,eyebrow:a,title:o,lede:s,metaDescription:c,date:null,hero:u,sections:d,cta:f,related:p,notes:m};export{f as cta,l as date,h as default,a as eyebrow,u as hero,s as lede,c as metaDescription,m as notes,t as path,r as practice,i as region,p as related,d as sections,n as sourceUrl,o as title,e as type};